diff --git a/docs/ACCESSIBILITY_LANGUAGES.md b/docs/ACCESSIBILITY_LANGUAGES.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3b338dd
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/ACCESSIBILITY_LANGUAGES.md
@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@
+# Accessibility and international text
+
+GUI.Forms retains one semantic tree. Native adapters expose its names, states, hierarchy, bounds and actions through AppKit accessibility on macOS, MSAA on Windows, and ATK/AT-SPI on the Linux X11 host. Application-specific drawing controls and document behavior belong to consumers.
+
+A containing focus scope narrows semantic snapshots and rejects actions outside its live root. Active popup owners can still receive collapse. This protects modal dialogs from stale assistive references to background controls. Native accessible objects become disconnected or defunct when the window or control disappears.
+
+Windows responds to WM_GETOBJECT with IAccessible objects. Snapshots are requested lazily after a client observes the window. Linux integrates the ATK bridge's GLib descriptors and deadlines into the existing wait loop; it does not add a redraw timer or GTK widgets. The bridge requires development packages providing pkg-config modules `atk` and `atk-bridge-2.0`.
+
+The private HarfBuzz text adapter uses unchanged SheenBidi 2.9.0 to order directional runs before font fallback and shaping. Logical UTF-8 offsets remain attached to glyphs. Mixed Hebrew/Arabic, Latin text and numbers have regression fixtures. Fallback line metrics contain the actual shaped glyph ink instead of reserving the tallest marks in an entire fallback family; this keeps compact interface lines usable. Unspaced labels wrap at grapheme boundaries.
+
+Additional original Noto font files cover Arabic, Hebrew, Devanagari, Bengali and Gurmukhi. Font source URLs and SHA-256 hashes are recorded in `assets/fonts/language-font-provenance.json`; the SIL OFL is included. SheenBidi source provenance and Apache license are under `third_party/sheenbidi/`.
+
+Validation includes the portable and macOS suites, a Windows COM fixture under Wine, and a separate AT-SPI client against a Linux VM fixture. Wine is not native Windows assistive-technology validation. The Linux fixture validates the service adapter without claiming full application/Orca usability. Neither bridge is a substitute for sessions with users of voice, switch, eye-tracking and screen-reader software.
